<!-- ==================== 3. AFFORDABILITY ANALYSIS ==================== -->
<div class="section" id="affordability-analysis">
  <h2>3. Affordability and Rent Burden Analysis</h2>

  <p>Housing affordability is the most direct measure of economic stress experienced by urban residents. The rent-to-salary ratio &mdash; what share of net monthly income is consumed by city-center one-bedroom rent &mdash; ranges from <strong>51.0%</strong> (Lahore) to <strong>71.2%</strong> (Seattle) across the dataset. Critically, no city in the dataset achieves the commonly cited 30% affordability threshold, indicating a <strong>universal global housing affordability crisis</strong>.</p>

  <div class="callout danger">
    <strong>Critical finding:</strong> All 80 cities have rent-to-salary ratios exceeding 50%. The global average of 61.5% means the typical urban worker spends nearly two-thirds of net income on rent alone, leaving minimal room for food, healthcare, transportation, and savings.
