<!-- ==================== 6. KEY FINDINGS ==================== -->
<div class="section" id="key-findings-recommendations">
  <h2>6. Key Findings and Policy Implications</h2>

  <h3>Principal Findings</h3>
  <ol class="findings" style="list-style-type: decimal; margin-left: 20px;">
    <li><strong>Universal rent burden:</strong> No city in the 80-city dataset achieves a rent-to-salary ratio below 50%. The global average of 61.5% indicates that housing affordability is a universal crisis, not confined to any single region or income bracket. Even the wealthiest cities (Swiss cities, Seattle, San Francisco) see ratios of 67&ndash;71%.</li>
    <li><strong>Purchasing power, not nominal cost, determines crisis severity:</strong> Tier 5 cities have low nominal costs (COL index 24&ndash;42) but purchasing power indices of 35&ndash;62, meaning residents can afford far less than cost levels suggest. Nominal cost comparisons without purchasing power adjustments are misleading for policy prioritization.</li>
    <li><strong>Inflation is the primary crisis accelerant:</strong> Tier 5 cities average 28.4% inflation vs. 1.4% in Tier 1. Among individual cities, Buenos Aires (150%), Beirut (50%), and Istanbul (47%) face inflation rates that erode living standards faster than any policy intervention can counteract.</li>
    <li><strong>Oil shock exposure correlates with economic fragility:</strong> The 23 high-exposure cities average 20.4% inflation and PPP of 67.8, compared to 3.4% inflation and PPP of 128.6 in the 25 low-exposure cities. Energy transition and diversification should be considered structural affordability interventions.</li>
    <li><strong>Regional inequality is extreme:</strong> Western European average salaries ($4,849) are nearly 10× those in South Asia ($494). North American purchasing power (132.2) is nearly 3× that of West Africa (46.0). These gaps are widening as inflation differentials compound.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Priority Cities Requiring Urgent Policy Attention</h3>
