  <h3>Policy Implications</h3>
  <ul class="findings">
    <li><strong>Housing supply interventions are universally needed.</strong> With all 80 cities above the 50% rent burden threshold, the crisis is structural and global. Targeted social housing, rent regulation, and zoning reform should be considered in every urban context, not just high-profile markets.</li>
    <li><strong>Inflation stabilization is the highest-impact intervention in Tier 5 cities.</strong> No supply-side housing reform can outpace 28&ndash;150% annual price increases. Monetary policy stabilization, potentially via international coordination, is a prerequisite for other interventions.</li>
    <li><strong>Energy diversification is an affordability strategy.</strong> The data shows that high oil shock exposure is a reliable predictor of economic fragility. Investments in renewable energy and reduced fossil fuel dependence should be framed as economic resilience measures, not solely environmental ones.</li>
    <li><strong>Purchasing power metrics should replace cost indices in policy frameworks.</strong> Ranking cities by nominal cost-of-living index alone would prioritize interventions for Swiss cities over Cairo or Dhaka. PPP-adjusted metrics better capture lived economic stress and should be the primary basis for resource allocation.</li>
    <li><strong>Regional outlier cities need differentiated strategies.</strong> Singapore, Tel Aviv, and Hong Kong face fundamentally different cost pressures than their regional peers. Blanket regional policies will fail to address the specific structural factors (financial center premiums, conflict zones, extreme housing markets) driving costs in these cities.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Areas for Further Research</h3>
  <ul class="findings">
    <li>Longitudinal tracking of rent-to-salary ratios to identify cities where affordability is deteriorating fastest.</li>
    <li>Disaggregation of housing cost data by income quintile, as city averages may mask extreme inequality within cities.</li>
    <li>Causal analysis of the oil shock exposure &rarr; inflation &rarr; purchasing power collapse pathway to identify intervention points.</li>
    <li>Comparative analysis of policy responses in cities that have improved their crisis tier classification over time.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

